<BaseLayout title="About the archive"><header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Purpose & method</p><h1>Understand the idea.<br/><em>Check the evidence.</em></h1></header><div class="prose"><p>This is an independent educational archive of the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel. It is built for curious readers who know the basics of supply and demand and want to understand more.</p><h2>A prize is a starting point</h2><p>An award often recognizes a research program, several publications, or decades of contributions. We identify the important works through official award materials and primary research, and explain how each relates to the recognition. A lecture is labeled as a lecture, and a book as a book.</p><h2>Intuition before notation</h2><p>Each full explanation should begin with a recognizable problem, explain the mechanism in ordinary language, and introduce equations with their assumptions and symbols defined. Examples are labeled as illustrations; they are not invented empirical results.</p><h2>Editorial states mean something</h2><p>Official catalogue facts can appear before an essay is written. Research moves through metadata-only, researched, draft, needs-review, reviewed, and published states. An article cannot be marked reviewed without an explicit reviewer record covering citations, mathematics, contributions, and rights. Publication also requires valid PDF and EPUB overview exports.</p><p>Drafts are visible for transparency, labeled on the page and in exports, and excluded from the full-text editorial search index. They are never counted as completed research. <a href={href('coverage/')}>The coverage ledger</a> gives the current state of every year.</p><h2>What is verified</h2><p>Official names, motivations, identities and prize shares come from saved Nobel Prize API responses. Work metadata is matched against publisher and Crossref records. Associations between awards and publications require explicit evidence, and a freely readable file does not by itself establish permission to redistribute it.</p><h2>Help improve the archive</h2><p>Source corrections, accessibility reports, and editorial review are welcome through the <a href="https://github.com/willtheorangeguy/econ-nobel/issues">project issue tracker</a>. Include a primary source and identify the year or work concerned.</p><h2>Independence</h2><p>This site has no official affiliation with the Nobel Foundation or the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences. Award quotations and metadata are attributed to the official source. Site-authored explanations are educational interpretations, with limitations and unfinished work explicitly recorded.</p></div></BaseLayout>
